  {
    "path": "SKILL.md",
    "content": "---\nname: web-research\ndescription: Search the web and extract content from URLs. Use for research tasks, finding documentation, gathering information, fact-checking, or any task requiring external knowledge.\nallowed-tools:\n  - webSearch\n  - extractContent\n  - fetch\n---\n\n# Web Research\n\nResearch topics on the web through search, content extraction, and API access. Supports both quick lookups and deep research workflows.\n\n## Permission Scopes\n\n| Tool | Required Scope |\n|------|----------------|\n| webSearch | `utilities:webSearch` |\n| extractContent | `utilities:extract` |\n| fetch | `utilities:fetch` |\n\n## Available Tools\n\n### webSearch\nSearch the web for information on any topic.\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `--query` (required): Search query string (1-500 chars)\n\n**Examples:**\n```\nwebSearch --query \"React 19 new features\"\nwebSearch --query \"PostgreSQL vs MySQL performance comparison 2024\"\nwebSearch --query \"site:github.com tanstack query examples\"\n```\n\n**Returns:** Array of results with title, url, and snippet for each match.\n\n### extractContent\nExtract readable text content from a web page URL.\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `--url` (required): Full URL to extract content from\n\n**Examples:**\n```\nextractContent --url \"https://react.dev/blog/2024/04/25/react-19\"\nextractContent --url \"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/index.html\"\n```\n\n**Returns:** Cleaned text content from the page, with HTML stripped.\n\n**Best for:** Articles, documentation, blog posts, text-heavy pages.\n\n### fetch\nMake HTTP requests to URLs. Use for APIs, JSON data, or raw file content.\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `--url` (required): Full URL to fetch\n- `--method` (optional): HTTP method - `\"GET\"` | `\"POST\"` | `\"PUT\"` | `\"DELETE\"` (default: `\"GET\"`)\n- `--headers` (optional): JSON object of headers\n- `--body` (optional): Request body (for POST/PUT)\n\n**Examples:**\n```\nfetch --url \"https://api.github.com/repos/facebook/react\"\nfetch --url \"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts\" --method \"POST\" --body ''{\"title\":\"Test\",\"body\":\"Content\"}''\nfetch --url \"https://api.example.com/data\" --headers ''{\"Authorization\":\"Bearer token\"}''\n```\n\n**Returns:** Response body content. For JSON APIs, returns parsed data.\n\n**Best for:** REST APIs, JSON endpoints, raw data files.\n\n## Research Approaches\n\n### Quick Lookup\nFor simple factual questions:\n```\nwebSearch --query \"your question\"\n```\n\n### Standard Research\nFor topic exploration:\n1. Search for overview\n2. Extract key sources\n3. Synthesize findings\n\n### Deep Research\nFor comprehensive analysis, see `references/deep-research-workflow.md`:\n1. Plan: Decompose question into sub-goals\n2. Search: Multiple queries per sub-goal\n3. Extract: Read full content from best sources\n4. Verify: Cross-reference facts across sources\n5. Synthesize: Combine with citations\n\n## Reference Documentation\n\n- `references/search-strategies.md` - Query formulation and optimization\n- `references/deep-research-workflow.md` - Iterative research methodology\n- `references/source-verification.md` - Fact-checking and validation\n- `references/content-extraction.md` - extractContent and fetch usage\n- `references/synthesis-patterns.md` - Combining sources effectively\n- `references/common-workflows.md` - Research patterns by use case\n"
  },
  {
    "path": "references/search-strategies.md",
    "content": "# Search Strategies\n\nEffective search is the foundation of good research. Use these strategies to find relevant, high-quality sources.\n\n## Query Formulation\n\n### Be Specific\nNarrow queries yield better results.\n\n| Too Broad | Better |\n|-----------|--------|\n| \"javascript\" | \"javascript async await error handling\" |\n| \"database\" | \"PostgreSQL index optimization B-tree\" |\n| \"react\" | \"React 19 server components migration guide\" |\n\n### Include Context\nAdd year, version, or domain for relevance:\n```\nwebSearch --query \"Next.js 14 app router authentication 2024\"\nwebSearch --query \"Python 3.12 new features type hints\"\n```\n\n### Use Search Operators\n\n| Operator | Purpose | Example |\n|----------|---------|--------|\n| `\"phrase\"` | Exact match | `\"cannot read property undefined\"` |\n| `site:` | Specific domain | `site:github.com tanstack query` |\n| `-term` | Exclude | `javascript tutorial -jquery` |\n| `filetype:` | File type | `API design guidelines filetype:pdf` |\n\n## Multi-Query Strategy\n\nFor complex topics, use multiple complementary queries:\n\n**Topic: \"Implementing OAuth2 in Node.js\"**\n\n```\n# Conceptual understanding\nwebSearch --query \"OAuth2 authorization flow explained\"\n\n# Implementation guidance\nwebSearch --query \"Node.js OAuth2 implementation tutorial 2024\"\n\n# Specific library\nwebSearch --query \"passport.js OAuth2 strategy example\"\n\n# Common issues\nwebSearch --query \"OAuth2 refresh token Node.js best practices\"\n```\n\n## Query Refinement\n\nIf initial results are poor:\n\n1. **Too few results**: Broaden terms, remove specifics\n2. **Too many irrelevant**: Add qualifiers, use exact phrases\n3. **Outdated results**: Add year or \"latest\"\n4. **Wrong domain**: Use `site:` operator\n\n## When to Use Each Tool\n\n| Scenario | Tool | Why |\n|----------|------|-----|\n| Find sources | webSearch | Discover relevant URLs |\n| Read articles | extractContent | Get clean text from pages |\n| API data | fetch | Direct data access |\n| JSON files | fetch | Raw content retrieval |\n| Documentation | extractContent | Readable format |\n\n## Source Quality Indicators\n\nPrioritize sources from:\n- Official documentation sites\n- Established tech blogs (major companies)\n- Academic/research institutions\n- High-reputation community sites (Stack Overflow, GitHub)\n- Recent publication dates\n\nBe cautious with:\n- Content farms (thin, SEO-optimized content)\n- Very old articles (may be outdated)\n- Anonymous sources without citations\n"
  },
  {
    "path": "references/deep-research-workflow.md",
    "content": "# Deep Research Workflow\n\nFor comprehensive research requiring multiple sources and verification, follow this iterative methodology.\n\n## Overview\n\n```\nPlan -> Search -> Extract -> Verify -> Synthesize\n  ^                                        |\n  +------------ Refine ------------------+\n```\n\n## Phase 1: Planning\n\nBefore searching, decompose the research question into structured sub-goals.\n\n**Example Question:** \"What are the best practices for building production LLM applications?\"\n\n**Sub-goals:**\n1. Architecture patterns for LLM apps\n2. Prompt engineering best practices\n3. Cost optimization strategies\n4. Monitoring and observability\n5. Security considerations\n6. Testing approaches\n\n**Why Plan First:**\n- Ensures comprehensive coverage\n- Prevents rabbit holes\n- Enables parallel research tracks\n- Makes synthesis easier\n\n## Phase 2: Search\n\nFor each sub-goal, generate multiple search queries:\n\n```\n# Sub-goal: Architecture patterns\nwebSearch --query \"LLM application architecture patterns 2024\"\nwebSearch --query \"production LLM system design\"\nwebSearch --query \"RAG architecture best practices\"\n```\n\n**Multi-Query Benefits:**\n- Different queries surface different sources\n- Reduces single-query bias\n- Catches varying terminology\n\n## Phase 3: Extract\n\nFrom search results, identify and extract the most promising sources:\n\n```\n# Evaluate search results, select best URLs\nextractContent --url \"https://example.com/llm-architecture-guide\"\nextractContent --url \"https://blog.company.com/production-llm-lessons\"\n```\n\n**Selection Criteria:**\n- Relevance to sub-goal\n- Source credibility\n- Publication date\n- Depth of coverage\n\n## Phase 4: Verify\n\nCross-reference key claims across multiple independent sources.\n\n**Verification Questions:**\n- Do multiple sources agree on this point?\n- Are there contradicting views?\n- Is this opinion or established fact?\n- What is the evidence quality?\n\n**Handling Contradictions:**\n1. Note the disagreement explicitly\n2. Check publication dates (newer may supersede)\n3. Consider source expertise\n4. Present both views if unresolved\n\nSee `references/source-verification.md` for detailed verification strategies.\n\n## Phase 5: Synthesize\n\nCombine findings into a coherent response:\n\n**Structure:**\n1. Executive summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Key findings by sub-goal\n3. Supporting evidence with citations\n4. Areas of uncertainty or debate\n5. Recommendations (if applicable)\n\n**Citation Format:**\n- Include source URLs for key claims\n- Note when information is from a single source\n- Distinguish between widely-agreed facts and opinions\n\nSee `references/synthesis-patterns.md` for detailed guidance.\n\n## Refinement Loop\n\nDuring any phase, you may need to refine:\n\n- **New questions emerge**: Add to sub-goals, return to Search\n- **Insufficient sources**: Try different queries\n- **Contradictions found**: Search for resolution\n- **Gaps identified**: Target missing areas\n\n## Time Management\n\n| Research Depth | Typical Effort |\n|----------------|----------------|\n| Quick lookup | 1-2 searches |\n| Standard research | 3-5 searches, 2-3 extractions |\n| Deep research | 10+ searches, 5+ extractions, verification |\n\n## Example: Complete Flow\n\n**Question:** \"How do I implement rate limiting in a Node.js API?\"\n\n```\n# 1. Plan: Sub-goals\n- Rate limiting algorithms (token bucket, sliding window)\n- Node.js implementation options (libraries vs custom)\n- Best practices and common pitfalls\n\n# 2. Search\nwebSearch --query \"rate limiting algorithms explained token bucket\"\nwebSearch --query \"Node.js rate limiting library comparison 2024\"\nwebSearch --query \"express rate limit best practices production\"\n\n# 3. Extract (from best results)\nextractContent --url \"https://blog.example.com/rate-limiting-deep-dive\"\nextractContent --url \"https://docs.library.com/rate-limiting\"\n\n# 4. Verify: Cross-check recommendations across sources\n\n# 5. Synthesize: Combine into actionable guidance\n```\n"
  },
  {
    "path": "references/source-verification.md",
    "content": "# Source Verification\n\nVerifying information across sources is critical for accurate research. Use these strategies to validate claims and handle contradictions.\n\n## Cross-Source Validation\n\n### The Three-Source Rule\nFor important claims, seek confirmation from at least three independent sources:\n\n```\n# Claim: \"React 19 improves hydration performance by 50%\"\n\n# Source 1: Official announcement\nwebSearch --query \"React 19 release announcement performance\"\n\n# Source 2: Independent benchmarks\nwebSearch --query \"React 19 hydration benchmark comparison\"\n\n# Source 3: Community validation\nwebSearch --query \"React 19 performance real world experience\"\n```\n\n### Independence Criteria\nSources are independent if they:\n- Come from different organizations\n- Use different methodologies\n- Don''t cite each other as primary source\n\n## Source Credibility Assessment\n\n### Credibility Hierarchy\n\n| Tier | Source Type | Trust Level |\n|------|-------------|-------------|\n| 1 | Official documentation, primary research | Highest |\n| 2 | Established tech publications, major company blogs | High |\n| 3 | Well-known community experts, reputable tutorials | Medium |\n| 4 | General blogs, forum posts, Q&A sites | Verify |\n| 5 | Anonymous sources, content farms | Low |\n\n### Red Flags\n- No author attribution\n- No publication date\n- Excessive ads or clickbait\n- Claims without citations\n- Outdated information presented as current\n\n## Handling Contradictions\n\nWhen sources disagree:\n\n### 1. Check Context\n```\n# Source A says: \"Use JWT for session management\"\n# Source B says: \"Never use JWT for sessions\"\n\n# Search for context\nwebSearch --query \"JWT session management pros cons debate\"\n```\n\nOften contradictions reflect different contexts or trade-offs.\n\n### 2. Check Dates\nNewer sources may reflect updated best practices:\n- Check publication dates\n- Look for \"this replaces previous advice\" language\n- Consider technology evolution\n\n### 3. Check Expertise\n- Who is the author?\n- What is their background in this area?\n- Is this their area of expertise?\n\n### 4. Present Both Views\nIf contradiction is unresolved:\n```\nThere are two schools of thought on this:\n\n**View A (Source 1, Source 2):** [Argument]\n**View B (Source 3):** [Counter-argument]\n\nThe choice depends on [specific factors].\n```\n\n## Fact-Checking Strategies\n\n### Statistics and Numbers\n```\n# Verify claimed statistics\nwebSearch --query \"[exact statistic] source original study\"\n```\n\nLook for:\n- Original research source\n- Methodology description\n- Sample size and conditions\n\n### Technical Claims\n```\n# Verify technical claims\nwebSearch --query \"[feature] official documentation\"\nfetch --url \"https://api.github.com/repos/[repo]\" # Check actual implementation\n```\n\n### Historical Claims\n```\n# Verify historical claims\nwebSearch --query \"[event] [year] original announcement\"\n```\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\nBefore including a claim in your synthesis:\n\n- [ ] Is the source credible?\n- [ ] Is the information current?\n- [ ] Can it be verified elsewhere?\n- [ ] Are there notable contradictions?\n- [ ] Is context properly represented?\n\n## When to Note Uncertainty\n\nExplicitly flag when:\n- Only one source found\n- Sources conflict without resolution\n- Information may be outdated\n- Claim is opinion vs. established fact\n- Original source couldn''t be located\n\n**Example:**\n```\nAccording to [Source], the recommended approach is X. \nNote: This is based on a single source and may reflect \none perspective rather than industry consensus.\n```\n"
  },
  {
    "path": "references/content-extraction.md",
    "content": "# Content Extraction\n\nGuidance on using extractContent and fetch effectively for different content types.\n\n## extractContent\n\nUse for human-readable web pages where you need the text content.\n\n### Best Use Cases\n\n| Content Type | Effectiveness | Notes |\n|--------------|---------------|-------|\n| Blog posts | Excellent | Clean text extraction |\n| Documentation | Excellent | Preserves structure |\n| News articles | Good | May include some navigation |\n| Landing pages | Fair | Marketing content mixed in |\n| SPAs | Variable | May miss dynamic content |\n\n### Examples\n\n```\n# Documentation\nextractContent --url \"https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html\"\n\n# Blog post\nextractContent --url \"https://blog.example.com/understanding-react-hooks\"\n\n# Technical article\nextractContent --url \"https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservices.html\"\n```\n\n### Limitations\n\n- **JavaScript-heavy sites**: May not capture dynamically loaded content\n- **Login-required content**: Cannot access authenticated pages\n- **PDFs**: Not supported (use fetch for raw PDF if needed)\n- **Very long pages**: Content may be truncated\n\n### Tips\n\n1. **Check the URL first**: Use webSearch to find the most relevant page\n2. **Try the canonical URL**: Avoid mobile or AMP versions\n3. **Look for text-focused pages**: Documentation > Landing pages\n\n## fetch\n\nUse for APIs, raw data, or when you need full control over the request.\n\n### API Requests\n\n```\n# GET request (default)\nfetch --url \"https://api.github.com/repos/facebook/react\"\n\n# With headers\nfetch --url \"https://api.example.com/data\" --headers ''{\"Accept\":\"application/json\"}''\n\n# POST request\nfetch --url \"https://api.example.com/search\" --method \"POST\" --body ''{\"query\":\"test\"}'' --headers ''{\"Content-Type\":\"application/json\"}''\n```\n\n### Raw File Access\n\n```\n# JSON file\nfetch --url \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/user/repo/main/package.json\"\n\n# Text file\nfetch --url \"https://example.com/data.csv\"\n\n# Configuration\nfetch --url \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/user/repo/main/.eslintrc.json\"\n```\n\n### Common APIs\n\n| API | Example |\n|-----|--------|\n| GitHub | `fetch --url \"https://api.github.com/repos/{owner}/{repo}\"` |\n| npm | `fetch --url \"https://registry.npmjs.org/{package}\"` |\n| PyPI | `fetch --url \"https://pypi.org/pypi/{package}/json\"` |\n\n## Choosing Between Tools\n\n| Need | Use | Why |\n|------|-----|-----|\n| Read an article | extractContent | Cleans HTML, returns text |\n| Get JSON data | fetch | Returns parsed JSON |\n| Check API response | fetch | Full control over request |\n| Read documentation | extractContent | Readable text format |\n| Download raw file | fetch | Direct content access |\n| Scrape structured data | fetch + parse | More control needed |\n\n## Error Handling\n\n### Common Issues\n\n| Error | Cause | Solution |\n|-------|-------|----------|\n| 403 Forbidden | Blocked by site | Try different source |\n| 404 Not Found | URL changed/removed | Search for current URL |\n| Timeout | Slow/unresponsive | Try again or use alternative |\n| Empty content | JS-rendered page | Try fetch or find static version |\n\n### Fallback Strategy\n\n```\n# If extractContent fails, try:\n1. webSearch for alternative source\n2. fetch for raw content (if applicable)\n3. Look for cached/archived version\n```\n\n## Rate Limiting Considerations\n\n- Space out multiple requests\n- Don''t repeatedly hit the same URL\n- Respect robots.txt and ToS\n- Use search to find relevant pages first, then extract selectively\n"
  },
  {
    "path": "references/synthesis-patterns.md",
    "content": "# Synthesis Patterns\n\nCombining information from multiple sources into coherent, well-cited responses.\n\n## Core Principles\n\n### 1. Ground Everything in Evidence\nEvery significant claim should trace back to a source:\n\n**Good:**\n```\nReact 19 introduces a new compiler that automatically \noptimizes re-renders (Source: React team blog post).\n```\n\n**Avoid:**\n```\nReact 19 is much faster than previous versions.\n```\n\n### 2. Distinguish Fact from Opinion\n\n**Fact (verifiable):**\n```\nTypeScript 5.0 added the ''satisfies'' operator (TypeScript release notes).\n```\n\n**Opinion (attributed):**\n```\nAccording to the author, this approach is preferred \nbecause it provides better type inference.\n```\n\n### 3. Acknowledge Uncertainty\n\n```\nBased on available sources, the recommended approach is X. \nHowever, this may vary depending on specific requirements.\n```\n\n## Report Structures\n\n### Quick Summary (1-2 paragraphs)\n\n```\n[Direct answer to the question]\n\n[Key supporting points with sources]\n```\n\n### Standard Research Response\n\n```\n## Summary\n[2-3 sentence overview]\n\n## Key Findings\n- Finding 1 (Source)\n- Finding 2 (Source)\n- Finding 3 (Source)\n\n## Details\n[Expanded explanation]\n\n## Sources\n- [Source 1 title](URL)\n- [Source 2 title](URL)\n```\n\n### Deep Research Report\n\n```\n## Executive Summary\n[Brief overview of findings]\n\n## Background\n[Context and why this matters]\n\n## Findings by Topic\n\n### Topic 1\n[Detailed findings with citations]\n\n### Topic 2\n[Detailed findings with citations]\n\n## Analysis\n[Synthesis across topics, patterns identified]\n\n## Recommendations\n[Actionable takeaways]\n\n## Limitations\n[What wasn''t covered, uncertainties]\n\n## Sources\n[Full list of sources used]\n```\n\n## Citation Practices\n\n### Inline Citations\n\n```\nThe framework supports server-side rendering out of the box \n(Next.js Documentation). Performance benchmarks show 40% \nfaster initial load times compared to client-side rendering \n(Web.dev Performance Study, 2024).\n```\n\n### Source List\n\n```\n## Sources\n- [Next.js Documentation - Rendering](https://nextjs.org/docs/rendering)\n- [Web.dev - Server-side rendering](https://web.dev/ssr)\n- [React Blog - React 19 RC](https://react.dev/blog/2024/react-19-rc)\n```\n\n### When Single-Source\n\n```\nNote: This information comes primarily from [Source]. \nIndependent verification was not available.\n```\n\n## Handling Multiple Perspectives\n\n### Agreement\n```\nSources consistently recommend X approach:\n- Source A emphasizes [aspect 1]\n- Source B highlights [aspect 2]\n- Source C confirms [aspect 3]\n```\n\n### Disagreement\n```\nThere are differing views on this topic:\n\n**Approach A** (Source 1, Source 2):\n[Description and reasoning]\n\n**Approach B** (Source 3):\n[Description and reasoning]\n\n**Recommendation:** [Your synthesis based on the user''s context]\n```\n\n## Avoiding Common Pitfalls\n\n### Hallucination Prevention\n- Don''t add details not in sources\n- Don''t extrapolate beyond evidence\n- Say \"I couldn''t find information on X\" if needed\n\n### Cherry-Picking Prevention\n- Include contradicting evidence\n- Note limitations of sources\n- Present balanced view\n\n### Over-Generalization Prevention\n- Qualify scope of claims\n- Note when something applies to specific contexts\n- Avoid \"always\" and \"never\" without evidence\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\nBefore finalizing synthesis:\n\n- [ ] All major claims have sources\n- [ ] Facts and opinions are distinguished\n- [ ] Contradictions are addressed\n- [ ] Uncertainties are noted\n- [ ] Sources are listed\n- [ ] Structure is appropriate for depth requested\n"
  },
  {
    "path": "references/common-workflows.md",
    "content": "# Common Research Workflows\n\nPractical patterns for different research scenarios.\n\n## Quick Fact Lookup\n\n**Goal:** Get a specific answer quickly.\n\n```\n# Single query, scan results\nwebSearch --query \"Node.js current LTS version 2024\"\n\n# If needed, extract for details\nextractContent --url \"https://nodejs.org/en/about/releases\"\n```\n\n**Output:** Direct answer with source.\n\n## How-To Research\n\n**Goal:** Learn how to implement something.\n\n```\n# 1. Find tutorials\nwebSearch --query \"how to implement [feature] [technology] tutorial\"\n\n# 2. Find official docs\nwebSearch --query \"[technology] [feature] documentation\"\n\n# 3. Extract best sources\nextractContent --url \"[tutorial-url]\"\nextractContent --url \"[docs-url]\"\n\n# 4. Check for common pitfalls\nwebSearch --query \"[feature] [technology] common mistakes\"\n```\n\n**Output:** Step-by-step guidance with code examples.\n\n## Technology Comparison\n\n**Goal:** Compare options for a decision.\n\n```\n# 1. Find comparison articles\nwebSearch --query \"[option A] vs [option B] comparison 2024\"\n\n# 2. Get each option''s perspective\nwebSearch --query \"[option A] advantages best use cases\"\nwebSearch --query \"[option B] advantages best use cases\"\n\n# 3. Check real-world experiences\nwebSearch --query \"[option A] vs [option B] production experience\"\n\n# 4. Extract key sources\nextractContent --url \"[comparison-article]\"\n```\n\n**Output:** Comparison table with pros/cons and recommendation.\n\n## Bug/Error Investigation\n\n**Goal:** Understand and fix an error.\n\n```\n# 1. Search exact error message\nwebSearch --query \"\\\"exact error message here\\\"\"\n\n# 2. Add technology context\nwebSearch --query \"[error message] [technology] solution\"\n\n# 3. Check official issues\nwebSearch --query \"site:github.com [error] [repository]\"\n\n# 4. Extract solution details\nextractContent --url \"[stackoverflow-or-github-issue]\"\n```\n\n**Output:** Explanation of cause and fix.\n\n## API/Library Documentation\n\n**Goal:** Understand how to use an API or library.\n\n```\n# 1. Find official docs\nwebSearch --query \"[library] official documentation\"\n\n# 2. Get API reference\nfetch --url \"https://api.[service].com/docs/openapi.json\"\n\n# 3. Find examples\nwebSearch --query \"[library] [specific feature] example\"\n\n# 4. Extract docs\nextractContent --url \"[docs-url]\"\n```\n\n**Output:** API usage guide with examples.\n\n## Comprehensive Topic Research\n\n**Goal:** Deep understanding of a complex topic.\n\n```\n# 1. Plan sub-topics (see deep-research-workflow.md)\n\n# 2. For each sub-topic:\nwebSearch --query \"[sub-topic] overview explained\"\nwebSearch --query \"[sub-topic] best practices\"\nwebSearch --query \"[sub-topic] examples real world\"\n\n# 3. Extract key sources\nextractContent --url \"[authoritative-source-1]\"\nextractContent --url \"[authoritative-source-2]\"\nextractContent --url \"[authoritative-source-3]\"\n\n# 4. Verify key claims (see source-verification.md)\n\n# 5. Synthesize (see synthesis-patterns.md)\n```\n\n**Output:** Comprehensive report with multiple sections.\n\n## Current Events / News\n\n**Goal:** Get up-to-date information.\n\n```\n# 1. Search with date context\nwebSearch --query \"[topic] [current month] [year]\"\nwebSearch --query \"[topic] latest news\"\n\n# 2. Find multiple news sources\nextractContent --url \"[news-source-1]\"\nextractContent --url \"[news-source-2]\"\n\n# 3. Verify across sources\n```\n\n**Output:** Summary of current state with multiple sources.\n\n## Code Example Search\n\n**Goal:** Find working code examples.\n\n```\n# 1. Search GitHub\nwebSearch --query \"site:github.com [feature] [language] example\"\n\n# 2. Search documentation\nwebSearch --query \"[library] [feature] code example\"\n\n# 3. Fetch raw code if needed\nfetch --url \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/[user]/[repo]/main/[file]\"\n```\n\n**Output:** Working code with explanation.\n\n## Package/Dependency Research\n\n**Goal:** Evaluate a package before using it.\n\n```\n# 1. Get package info\nfetch --url \"https://registry.npmjs.org/[package]\"\nfetch --url \"https://api.github.com/repos/[owner]/[repo]\"\n\n# 2. Check community sentiment\nwebSearch --query \"[package] review pros cons\"\nwebSearch --query \"[package] vs alternatives\"\n\n# 3. Check recent activity\nwebSearch --query \"[package] [current year] maintained\"\n```\n\n**Output:** Package evaluation with metrics and recommendation.\n"
  }
